g NoSuchArgumentError exceptions when trying to get arguments by position, falling back to keyword inference.\nS5. If no mode argument is found at all, it adds a message indicating an unspecified encoding with high confidence and returns early.\nS6. If the mode is invalid, it adds a 'bad-open-mode' message with the mode value and the current confidence level.\nS7. StdlibChecker._check_open_call performs various checks for an open call.\nS8. The function checks if the value of the encoding argument is None. If so, it adds another 'unspecified-encoding' message with the current confidence level.\nS9. The function checks if the function name is in OPEN_FILES_MODE and if the mode value is valid using the _check_mode_str helper function.\nS10. _check_open_call is a method of the StdlibChecker class.\nS11. The function attempts to retrieve the mode argument from the call by position first.\nS12. If no encoding is found by position, it infers it using a keyword search.\nS13. The function checks for an encoding argument. For pathlib methods like read_text, write_text, or others, it retrieves the encoding from different positions based on the method name.\n\nFor each statement decide:\n- supported: the shown code makes the statement true (a statement about purpose/intent counts as supported unless the code contradicts it).\n- contradicted: the shown code clearly makes the statement false, e.g. a named parameter, call, attribute, return value, exception or condition it describes no longer exists or now behaves differently.\n- unresolved: the statement concerns code that is not shown (callers, helper internals, external systems) and the shown code neither confirms nor refutes it.\nBe strict about \"contradicted\": require concrete evidence in the shown code or the diff; renaming a local variable or reformatting does not contradict a statement about behaviour.\n\nAnswer with exactly one line per statement and nothing else, in this format:\nS1: supported\nS2: contradicted -- <ev